{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>Take for yourself spices, etc.</b> He counted the galbanum among the ingredients of the incense to teach us to include the transgressors of Israel in every fast (Keritot 6b), and the reason for this is that if the transgressors of Israel were not included in the fast, there is reason to fear that the fast would not be a mitzvah but rather a transgression, and it would not ascend for acceptance. For we find (Ta'anit 11a) that the nazirite is called a sinner because he withheld himself from wine — but if there are among us transgressors of Israel, then in any case we must fast on their account, for all Israel are guarantors for one another. And through this the matter is well settled: whence do we derive that the inclusion of galbanum alludes to the inclusion of the transgressors specifically in a fast? And that it is counted among the ingredients of the incense specifically? It is because through fasts and repentance, the willful sins are transformed into merits, as it is written (Isaiah 40:2), ״כִּי נִרְצָה עֲוֹנָהּ״ — that even the sin itself ascends for acceptance, as a pleasing fragrance. And our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Sanhedrin 37a): ״כְּפֶלַח הָרִמּוֹן רַקָּתֵךְ״ — even the empty ones among you are as full of mitzvot as a pomegranate. And this is difficult: if they are full of mitzvot as a pomegranate, then why did He call them 'empty'? Rather, it is certain that they spoke this of repentance, for every empty act that is in them — that is, the sin — from it they will be filled with mitzvot, for the willful sins and the emptiness are transformed into merits, and even the sin ascends as a pleasing fragrance like the mitzvot, after the pattern of the galbanum, which ascends as a pleasing fragrance like all the other spices. From here they learned to say that this matter alludes to the fast and to repentance, and this is a precious interpretation."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:24:31.741561071Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}